em>).","id":75771,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:38070","name":"anti-arrhythmia drug","definition":"A drug used for the treatment or prevention of cardiac arrhythmias. Anti-arrhythmia drugs may affect the polarisation-repolarisation phase of the action potential, its excitability or refractoriness, or impulse conduction or membrane responsiveness within cardiac fibres.","id":38070,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:77746","name":"human metabolite","definition":"Any mammalian metabolite  produced during a metabolic reaction in humans (<em>Homo sapiens</em>).","id":77746,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35705","name":"immunosuppressive agent","definition":"An agent that suppresses immune function by one of several mechanisms of action. Classical cytotoxic immunosuppressants act by inhibiting DNA synthesis. Others may act through activation of T-cells or by inhibiting the activation of helper cells. In addition, an immunosuppressive agent is a role played by a compound which is exhibited by a capability to diminish the extent and/or voracity of an immune response.","id":35705,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35469","name":"antidepressant","definition":"Antidepressants are mood-stimulating drugs used primarily in the treatment of affective disorders and related conditions.","id":35469,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":true,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:39142","name":"Bronsted base","definition":"A molecular entity capable of accepting a hydron from a donor (Brønsted acid).","id":39142,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":true,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:39141","name":"Bronsted acid","definition":"A molecular entity capable of donating a hydron to an acceptor (Brønsted base).","id":39141,"biologic